  <!-- STUDY GUIDE -->

  <section id="guide" class="panel active">
    <h2 class="section-heading">Study Guide</h2>
    <p class="intro">Review each topic and make sure you understand how the concepts apply to real economic situations.</p>

    <article class="topic">
      <h3>1. Bank of Canada and Interest Rates</h3>
      <p>The <span class="key">Bank of Canada</span> is Canada's central bank. One of its main responsibilities is keeping inflation low and stable, with a target of 2% inflation.</p>
      <p>It uses its policy interest rate to influence borrowing, saving, and spending throughout the economy.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="key">When interest rates increase:</span> Borrowing becomes more expensive, people and businesses may spend less, and inflation can slow down.</li>
        <li><span class="key">When interest rates decrease:</span> Borrowing becomes cheaper, encouraging spending and investment. This can help economic growth.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="example">
        <strong>Example:</strong> If the Bank of Canada raises interest rates, a family might delay buying a house because mortgage payments would be higher.
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="topic">
      <h3>2. Cost of Living and Purchasing Power</h3>
      <p>The <span class="key">cost of living</span> is the amount of money needed to afford everyday expenses such as housing, food, transportation, and utilities.</p>
      <p><span class="key">Purchasing power</span> is how much goods and services someone can buy with their money.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>If prices rise faster than income, purchasing power decreases.</li>
        <li>If income rises faster than prices, purchasing power increases.</li>
        <li>Inflation makes the same amount of money buy fewer goods and services over time.</li>
      </ul>
      <div class="example">
        <strong>Example:</strong> If someone's pay increases by 2% but prices increase by 5%, they have less purchasing power even though they earn more money.
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="topic">
      <h3>3. Businesses and Borrowing</h3>
      <p>Businesses often borrow money to expand, purchase equipment, hire employees, or build new facilities.</p>
      <p>Interest rates affect how expensive it is for a business to borrow money.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Higher interest rates increase the cost of loans.</li>
        <li>Businesses may delay or cancel projects if borrowing becomes too expensive.</li>
        <li>Lower interest rates can encourage investment and expansion.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><span class="key">Economic consequences:</span> If businesses reduce investment, there may be fewer jobs created, less construction, and slower economic growth.</p>
      <div class="example">
        <strong>Example:</strong> A construction company may decide not to build an apartment complex if higher loan payments make the project unprofitable.
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="topic">
      <h3>4. Consumer Price Index (CPI)</h3>
      <p>The <span class="key">Consumer Price Index (CPI)</span> measures changes in the average price of a basket of goods and services commonly purchased by Canadian households.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>CPI is used to measure inflation.</li>
        <li>If CPI increases, the average price level has increased.</li>
        <li>If CPI decreases, the average price level has decreased.</li>
        <li>A lower inflation rate does not necessarily mean prices are going down. It means prices are increasing more slowly.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><span class="key">Inflation rate:</span> The percentage change in the general price level over a period of time.</p>
      <div class="example">
        <strong>Example:</strong> If CPI rises from 150 to 153, the price level has increased by 2%.
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="topic">
      <h3>5. Factory Closures and Unemployment</h3>
      <p>When a factory closes, the workers who lose their jobs may contribute to an increase in unemployment.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="key">Unemployment:</span> People who are able and willing to work, are looking for work, but do not have a job.</li>
        <li>Families may have less income and reduce their spending.</li>
        <li>Local businesses may lose customers.</li>
        <li>Government spending on employment support may increase, while tax revenue may decrease.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Factory closures can also affect suppliers and other businesses connected to the factory.</p>
      <div class="example">
        <strong>Example:</strong> If a major factory closes in a small town, restaurants and stores may also lose business because fewer workers have income to spend.
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="topic">
      <h3>6. Trade Surplus and Trade Deficit</h3>
      <p>International trade involves countries buying and selling goods and services with each other.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="key">Exports:</span> Goods and services sold to other countries.</li>
        <li><span class="key">Imports:</span> Goods and services purchased from other countries.</li>
        <li><span class="key">Trade surplus:</span> The value of exports is greater than the value of imports.</li>
        <li><span class="key">Trade deficit:</span> The value of imports is greater than the value of exports.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>A trade surplus can support domestic production and employment. A trade deficit means a country is buying more from other countries than it is selling to them.</p>
      <p>A trade deficit is not automatically bad. It can reflect strong domestic demand or access to products and resources that are not produced locally.</p>
      <div class="example">
        <strong>Example:</strong> If Canada exports $80 billion worth of goods and imports $65 billion, it has a $15 billion trade surplus.
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="topic">
      <h3>7. GDP and Standard of Living</h3>
      <p><span class="key">Gross Domestic Product (GDP)</span> is the total market value of all final goods and services produced within a country during a specific period.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>GDP growth usually means the economy is producing more goods and services.</li>
        <li>GDP can increase when consumer spending, business investment, government spending, or exports increase.</li>
        <li>GDP per capita is GDP divided by the population.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><span class="key">Standard of living</span> refers to the material well-being of people, including their access to goods, services, income, and necessities.</p>
      <p>GDP is useful, but it does not measure everything. It does not directly show income inequality, happiness, environmental quality, or how wealth is distributed.</p>
      <div class="example">
        <strong>Example:</strong> A country can have a high GDP, but if most of its wealth goes to a small percentage of the population, many people may still have a low standard of living.
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="topic">
      <h3>8. Oil Prices and the Canadian Economy</h3>
      <p>Oil is an important Canadian export, especially for provinces such as Alberta, Saskatchewan, and Newfoundland and Labrador.</p>
      <p>Oil prices are affected by supply and demand.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="key">Higher oil prices:</span> Can increase revenue for oil producers, support jobs, and increase government royalties and tax revenue.</li>
        <li><span class="key">Lower oil prices:</span> Can reduce profits, lead to job losses, and decrease investment in the energy sector.</li>
        <li>Higher oil prices can also increase transportation and production costs for businesses and raise costs for consumers.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Oil prices can also affect the Canadian dollar because oil is a major Canadian export.</p>
      <div class="example">
        <strong>Example:</strong> If global oil demand increases, Canadian oil producers may earn more money. However, consumers may also pay more for gasoline and goods that require transportation.
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="topic">
      <h3>9. Inflation, Unemployment, and GDP Together</h3>
      <p>These are three important indicators used to understand the health of an economy.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="key">Inflation:</span> Measures how quickly the general price level is increasing.</li>
        <li><span class="key">Unemployment:</span> Measures the percentage of the labour force that is unemployed and looking for work.</li>
        <li><span class="key">GDP:</span> Measures the value of goods and services produced in the economy.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>These indicators can influence each other.</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Rapid economic growth can increase demand and sometimes contribute to inflation.</li>
        <li>Slow economic growth can lead businesses to reduce hiring or lay off workers.</li>
        <li>High inflation can reduce purchasing power and make everyday life more expensive.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><span class="key">Recession:</span> A significant decline in economic activity that lasts for an extended period. It often involves falling GDP and rising unemployment.</p>
      <div class="example">
        <strong>Example:</strong> If Canada's GDP falls and unemployment rises, it could indicate that the economy is weakening. The Bank of Canada may consider lowering interest rates to encourage spending and investment, depending on inflation conditions.
      </div>
    </article>

    <article class="topic">
      <h3>10. Currency Values and Exchange Rates</h3>
      <p>An <span class="key">exchange rate</span> is the value of one country's currency compared with another country's currency.</p>
      <p>A currency can appreciate or depreciate.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><span class="key">Appreciation:</span> A currency increases in value compared with another currency.</li>
        <li><span class="key">Depreciation:</span> A currency decreases in value compared with another currency.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>Factors that influence currency values include:</p>
      <ul>
        <li>Interest rates and investment opportunities.</li>
        <li>Supply and demand for a country's currency.</li>
        <li>Economic growth and confidence in the economy.</li>
        <li>Political stability and global events.</li>
        <li>Commodity prices, especially oil for Canada.</li>
      </ul>
      <p><span class="key">Effects of a stronger Canadian dollar:</span> Imports become cheaper for Canadians, but Canadian exports become more expensive for foreign buyers.</p>
      <p><span class="key">Effects of a weaker Canadian dollar:</span> Imports become more expensive, but Canadian exports may become cheaper for foreign buyers.</p>
      <div class="example">
        <strong>Example:</strong> If the Canadian dollar depreciates against the US dollar, Canadians generally need more Canadian dollars to purchase the same US product.
      </div>
    </article>
  </section>

